nd dosage</td><td>Using too much can be dangerous</td></tr><tr><td>FDA registration</td><td>Shows the product passed safety standards in the Philippines</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Spotting false claims</h4><ul><li>Promises like "cures all diseases" or "lose 10 kg in a week"</li><li>Testimonials only, with no scientific evidence</li><li>Pressure to "buy now" before a deadline</li></ul><blockquote><p><strong>Remember</strong></p><p>Food supplements are not medicine. Products labeled "No approved therapeutic claims" cannot claim to cure or treat illness.</p></blockquote><h3>Activity</h3><p>Part A: Plan a one-day active recreation outing for your family, listing the activity, schedule, things to bring, and safety rules. Part B: Examine one health product at home and record its expiration date, main ingredients, dosage, and whether it has an FDA registration number.</p><h3>Quick check</h3><ol><li>Give one mental benefit of active recreation.</li><li>Why should you check the FDA registration of a product?</li><li>Give one sign of a false health claim.</li></ol><details><summary>Show answers</summary><ol><li>Any one: reduces stress, improves mood.</li><li>It shows the product passed safety standards.</li><li>Any one: promises a cure for everything, unrealistic results, testimonials only, pressure to buy now.</li></ol></details>', 1 FROM courses c
